{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Recorded the contrast that explains why the finding is about classification rather than about unauthenticated messages in general — the question a reviewer is most likely to ask. Step treats a message with no term as a local message and skips every term comparison, then dispatches on type; for MsgApp, MsgHeartbeat and MsgSnap that reaches stepFollower, which zeroes the election timer and adopts m.From as leader before handling the payload (raft.go 1094-1100 and 1730-1739). So term-zero trust is not specific to MsgForgetLeader: what is specific is that it is the only locally-produced type missing from the inbound filter, letting it reach that trust from a peer with no illegal field at all, whereas a peer would have to send MsgApp or MsgHeartbeat with an illegal zero term to reach the same state change — a path no correct peer produces, bounded in effect (a follower's advertised leader and timer, with a divergent uncommitted tail that the next real leader truncates) and needing no classification change to describe. Recorded as a Behavior with its conditions and the four already-cited source ranges; no new defect is claimed for the illegal-term path, and validation passed with no execution requested.\"}"}}
